      sources: "Sources: Learn, Microsoft Graph national cloud deployments; Compare Azure Government and global Azure; Create federatedIdentityCredential; List signIns. Chapter 05, “Which cloud.”",
      say: `Next, which cloud. This chapter talks to three hosts: Microsoft Graph for app registrations and sign-ins, Resource Manager for managed identities, and the sign-in host for tokens. The estate is a G C C tenant with commercial Azure, so all three are the worldwide endpoints. G C C High and the Defense Department clouds have their own hosts, and the code switches only for those. One gap worth knowing: the managed identity pages the chapter read don't say whether they're available in Azure Government, which is fine here because this estate's Azure is commercial.`,
      detail: `The target estate is Microsoft 365 GCC with commercial Azure: Graph at graph.microsoft.com, Resource Manager at management.azure.com, sign-in at login.microsoftonline.com. GCC High and DoD use graph.microsoft.us and dod-graph.microsoft.us, Azure Government's management.usgovcloudapi.net, and login.microsoftonline.us. Creating a federated credential on an application and listing sign-ins are both available in US Government L4 and L5. The Resource Manager pages read for user-assigned identities don't state Azure Government availability; this estate's Azure is commercial, so the code sends them to management.azure.com for gcc, and to Azure Government only for gcchigh and dod. The token exchange test goes only to the fake, but the request it builds is addressed to the cloud's own sign-in host.`,
