      sources: "Sources: examples/non-person-identity (module, call list and fixtures). Chapter 05, “The code: what examples/non-person-identity adds,” “The inputs.”",
      say: `So what does the code add? A single new module and one entry script, which builds a plan by default and only writes when you hand it an approved plan. It reuses the shared cloud table and hashing from the earlier chapters but keeps its own client and fakes, so nothing earlier changes. Two files drive it. The conversion file, kept by identity engineering and reviewed with the application owners, says what each legacy credential becomes and which workload will present the token. And the cutover log, kept by the owner, records when each workload switched and who approved the disable.`,
      detail: `This chapter's code is powershell/NpiFederation/NpiFederation.psm1, one entry script, Invoke-NpiFederation.ps1 (plan by default; the write path with -PlanPath), its own call list, config/calls-ch05.json, and the fixtures in tests/fixtures/ch05. It shares NpiCore's cloud table, host check, findings and hashes, and keeps its own client, fakes and manifest, so Chapters 01 and 02 are untouched. Inputs: conversions.json, kept by identity engineering, reviewed with the application owners: one entry per legacy credential: wave, risk, owner; the legacy account or secret; what the host allows (azureHosted, oidcIssuer); the target (a user-assigned or system-assigned managed identity, an app registration's federated credentials, or a certificate); and for each federated credential its name, issuer, subject, audience and the workload whose issuer will present the token (github with repository, entity and value; kubernetes with issuer, namespace and service account; or a capturedSubject from a test run). cutover-log.csv, kept by the application owner: ConversionId, CutoverOn (the day the workload switched), RecordedBy, DisableApprovedBy (the owner's approval after a clean run), DisabledOn.`,
      cite: [],
