      sources: "Sources: Learn, Important considerations for federated identity credentials (propagation); List signIns (beta). Chapter 05, “Lab 2: test tenant.”",
      say: `Lab 2 moves to a test tenant, never production. You set up one small conversion: a test app with a secret, a test service account, and a test repository whose workflow signs in to Azure. The key step is capturing the claims: have the workflow print the decoded issuer and subject of its token, never the token itself, because that's the truth the check compares against. Then plan, get a second person to approve the hash, apply, and watch a real exchange land in the sign-in log. Try a wrong subject on purpose, run the parallel run forward, and back it all out by hand.`,
      detail: `Use a test tenant in the worldwide or GCC cloud, a test subscription, a test Active Directory domain and a test repository, never production, with fictional names. 1 Make a test conversion: a test app registration with a client secret, an enabled test account (svc- prefix) in a test OU, and a test repository whose workflow signs in with Learn's Azure Login sample (pinned in examples/third_party/azure-login-oidc-sample). Write a two-entry conversions.json: the test app getting a GitHub federated credential for the repository's test environment, and a new test user-assigned identity with no credential. 2 Capture the claims: run the workflow once with a step that prints the decoded iss and sub of its OIDC token, never the token itself. Record them as capturedSubject and capturedIssuer, and record whether the iss ends with a slash (research gaps, row 41) and whether the repository uses the immutable subject format (row 43). 3 Plan with an app holding only Application.Read.All and AuditLog.Read.All and Reader on the test subscription, signed in as in Chapter 01: ./Invoke-NpiFederation.ps1 -Cloud gcc -TenantId <tenant-id> -Domain <test-domain> -ConversionsPath <conversions> -CutoverLogPath <cutover-log> -OutputPath out/federation-test. Have a second person review plan.json and pass you its SHA-256. 4 Dry-run, then apply, signed in as a person with Cloud Application Administrator and Managed Identity Contributor on the test resource group: Connect-MgGraph -Environment Global -TenantId <tenant-id> -Scopes Application.Read.All, Application.ReadWrite.All; Connect-AzAccount -Environment AzureCloud -Tenant <tenant-id>; ./Invoke-NpiFederation.ps1 -Cloud gcc -PlanPath out/federation-test/plan.json -ApprovedSha256 <sha> -Domain <test-domain> -OutputPath out/federation-test-apply -Apply. 5 Exchange for real: wait a few minutes for the credential to propagate, run the workflow, and confirm its sign-in appears in the beta sign-in log with clientCredentialType federatedIdentityCredential and the credential's ID. Then add a second test credential with a deliberately wrong subject, run the workflow against it, and record the error the token endpoint returns (research gaps, row 44). Record whether a sign-in with the old client secret shows its key ID (row 47). 6 Run the parallel run: record CutoverOn in the cutover log, plan again, and confirm ParallelRunning with one sign-in. To see the disable without waiting 30 days, run the plan with -AsOf 31 days later and the owner's approval recorded, review, and apply: the test account is disabled. 7 Back it out: enable the test account, remove the test credentials and delete the test identity, the test app registration and the test account by hand in the test tenant and domain (this code never deletes), and delete the out folders: they name accounts and identities.`,
