      sources: "Sources: Chapter 05, “Checklist”; “Continue the book.”",
      say: `Here's what done looks like. Every conversion followed the order or says why. Every federated credential was checked against claims captured from a real run, and passed its exchange test, before cutover. Nothing sits in the tenant that nobody reviewed. The plan was approved by someone other than the person who applied it. Every parallel run had thirty clean days, every disable has the owner's approval, and nothing was deleted, so the way back is still open. Next, Chapter 6 turns to group managed service accounts, delegated managed service accounts, and the Kerberos island. Thanks for watching.`,
      detail: `Checklist: every conversion's target follows the order, or has a recorded reason (NPI-WIF-01). Every federated credential's issuer and subject were checked against the claims captured from a real test run of its workload, and its token exchange test passed, before cutover. Every credential in the tenant on a converted app or identity is in the reviewed file, or its retirement is in review. Every user-assigned identity was created by the write path, and every managed identity's role assignments are in its record for Chapter 07. The plan's SHA-256 was approved by someone other than the person who applied it, and the write path ran as a person, delegated. Every cutover is in the cutover log, and every parallel run shows sign-ins on the new identity, no legacy use, and 30 days. Every disable has the owner's approval and a DisabledOn; every secret is removed through Chapter 04's change, after its run. Nothing was deleted by this code; rollback (re-enable, revert the configuration) is still possible. The pinned samples match their manifests, and every upstream commit since the pins has been reviewed. Next: Chapter 06 — gMSAs, dMSAs and the Kerberos Island.`,
